          "projection_start": 44,
          "projection_end": 53,
          "concept": "immersion",
          "rule": "catalog"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "unit_id": "tag:02YM:proof",
      "source_text": "The property of being an immersion is stable under base change,\nsee Schemes, Lemma <REF_0001>.\nThe property of being an immersion is Zariski local on the base.\nFinally, let\n<MATH_0001> be a surjective morphism of affine schemes,\nwhich is flat and locally of finite presentation.\nNote that <MATH_0002> is open by\nMorphisms, Lemma <REF_0002>.\nLet <MATH_0003> be a morphism.\nAssume that the base change <MATH_0004> is an immersion.\nIn particular we see that <MATH_0005> is locally closed.\nHence by Topology, Lemma <REF_0003>\nwe see that <MATH_0006>\nis locally closed. Let <MATH_0007> be\nthe closed subset <MATH_0008>.\nBy Topology, Lemma <REF_0004>\nagain we see that <MATH_0009> is closed in <MATH_0010>.\nHence we may apply Lemma <REF_0005>\nto the fpqc covering <MATH_0011>\nand conclude that <MATH_0012> is a closed\nimmersion. In other words, <MATH_0013> is an immersion.\nTherefore Lemma <REF_0006> applies and we win.\n",
