    {
      "unit_id": "tag:0H8J:statement",
      "source_text": "The property <MATH_0001>``<MATH_0002> is dominant''\nis fppf local on the base.\n",
      "node_kind": "lemma",
      "protected_nodes": {
        "MATH_0001": "$\\mathcal{P}(f) =$",
        "MATH_0002": "$f$"
      },
      "required_source_terms": []
    },
    {
      "unit_id": "tag:0H8J:proof",
      "source_text": "By Morphisms, Lemma <REF_0001>,\ndominant morphisms are preserved\nunder pullback by open morphisms, hence\nby flat morphisms locally of finite presentation\n(Morphisms, Lemma <REF_0002>).\nThe other direction is easier.\nIndeed, being dominant is clearly Zariski local on the base.\nNext, let <MATH_0001> be a surjective morphism of schemes\n(not necessarily flat or locally of finite\npresentation),\nand let <MATH_0002> be a morphism. Assume that the base change\n<MATH_0003> is dominant. Then <MATH_0004> is a composition\nof two dominant morphisms, hence dominant. Since this is also\nthe composition <MATH_0005>, it follows that <MATH_0006> is dominant.\n",
      "node_kind": "proof",
      "protected_nodes": {
